  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nPrecipitation tonight...\nA surface low will track into the area this evening, accompanied \nby a mid-level shortwave. The highest rain chances (70-90%) \naccompanying the low will be on the northern edge of the warm air\nthis afternoon across northern Wisconsin, mainly north of a line \nfrom Merrill to Oconto. Probabilistic guidance keeps a corridor of\n40-60% chance of exceeding a half an inch of rainfall for the \nnorthern areas. Precipitation potential will quickly taper off \nsouth of this line, with around 20-40% chance across most of the \nrest of the area. Still, would expect some short-lived showers to \nproduce some quick accumulations for central to east- central with\nmore measurable precipitation for northern Wisconsin. Some meager\nupper instability accompanying showers could prove sufficient to\nproduce an occasional rumble of thunder (10-20% chance), but \nexpect widespread thunderstorms to be unlikely.\n\nPrecipitation Towards the End of the Week... A larger phasing \nsystem is expected towards the end of the week as southern and \nnorthern streams merge. Phasing systems generally have much \nuncertainty in terms of timing but some general agreement has come\ninto alignment with the most recent forecast cycles. Rain will \nstart sometime in the late morning to afternoon hours and cross \nthe region into the overnight hours. How long the system remains \naround into Friday remains uncertain and will depend largely on \nwhether the phasing occurs overhead or to our east. On the shorter\nend, dry conditions could be back by Friday morning and on the \nlonger end some wrap around moisture could keep a few showers \naround into Friday afternoon. Both solutions do still suggest \nbringing enough cold air in on the backside of the system to \nproduce the first snowflakes across far north- central Wisconsin \nnear the Upper Peninsula border, but accumulations will generally \nbe brief and light. This system will also bring another period of \nwindy conditions for Friday.\n\nTemperatures...\nThe near-record temperatures today will be the warmest \ntemperatures in the forecast, with high temperatures seeing a\ndownward trend through the end of the week. Friday is expected to\nbe the coldest day, with highs in the upper 40s in the northwoods\nto middle 50s in the warmest areas. A small warmup trend is then\nin store for the region over the weekend into next week.",
